---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const title = 'About YouTube CPM Bot';
const description =
	'Learn about YouTube CPM Bot, its purpose, CPM and RPM insights, estimated earnings calculations, and tools for YouTube creators.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={title} description={description} image="/blog-placeholder-about.jpg" />
		<style>
			/* About page only — uses the site's existing design tokens
			   (--accent, --gray, --black, --box-shadow) and Atkinson font.
			   No global styles are changed. */
			.eyebrow {
				font-size: 0.75em;
				font-weight: 700;
				letter-spacing: 0.12em;
				text-transform: uppercase;
				color: var(--accent);
				margin-bottom: 0.5em;
			}
			.lead {
				font-size: 1.15em;
				color: rgb(var(--gray-dark));
			}
			.hero-image img {
				display: block;
				margin: 0 auto 2em;
				border-radius: 12px;
				box-shadow: var(--box-shadow);
			}
			section {
				margin-bottom: 2.5em;
			}
			.cards {
				display: grid;
				grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
				gap: 1em;
				margin-top: 1em;
			}
			.card {
				border: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
				border-radius: 12px;
				padding: 1em 1.1em;
				background: #fff;
			}
			.card h3 {
				font-size: 1.1em;
				margin-bottom: 0.4em;
			}
			.card p {
				margin: 0;
				font-size: 0.9em;
				color: rgb(var(--gray));
			}
			.mission {
				border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
				background: rgba(var(--gray-light), 35%);
				border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0;
				padding: 1em 1.2em;
			}
			.mission p {
				margin: 0;
			}
			.formula {
				border: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
				border-radius: 12px;
				padding: 1em 1.2em;
				background: rgba(var(--gray-light), 25%);
				text-align: center;
				font-weight: 700;
				color: rgb(var(--black));
			}
			.disclaimer {
				border: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
				border-radius: 12px;
				padding: 1em 1.2em;
				background: rgba(var(--gray-light), 25%);
			}
			.actions {
				display: flex;
				gap: 0.75em;
				flex-wrap: wrap;
				margin-top: 1.5em;
			}
			.btn {
				display: inline-block;
				padding: 0.45em 1.1em;
				border-radius: 999px;
				font-weight: 700;
				font-size: 0.85em;
				text-decoration: none;
				border: 2px solid var(--accent);
			}
			.btn-primary {
				background: var(--accent);
				color: #fff;
			}
			.btn-primary:hover {
				color: #fff;
				background: var(--accent-dark);
				border-color: var(--accent-dark);
			}
			.btn-ghost {
				color: var(--accent);
				background: transparent;
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<!-- Hero -->
			<section aria-labelledby="about-heading">
				<p class="eyebrow">About</p>
				<h1 id="about-heading">About YouTube CPM Bot</h1>
				<div class="hero-image">
					<img width={1020} height={510} src="/blog-placeholder-about.jpg" alt="" />
				</div>
				<p class="lead">
					YouTube CPM Bot is a platform designed to help YouTube creators understand CPM
					(Cost Per Mille) and RPM (Revenue Per Mille), explore estimated earning potential,
					and make more informed content and audience-targeting decisions.
				</p>
				<p>
					Monetisation terms can feel opaque — especially when two channels with similar view
					counts earn very differently. This site translates CPM and RPM into plain language
					and simple, transparent estimates, so you can focus on the content decisions behind
					the numbers. Start with the articles on our <a href="/blog">blog</a>, or read how the
					numbers work below.
				</p>
				<div class="actions">
					<a class="btn btn-primary" href="/blog">Read the blog</a>
					<a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#how-it-works">How it works</a>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Mission -->
			<section aria-labelledby="mission-heading">
				<h2 id="mission-heading">Our Mission</h2>
				<div class="mission">
					<p>
						To help YouTube creators make better decisions using accessible, understandable
						CPM insights and practical analytics. No jargon for its own sake and no inflated
						promises — just clear explanations and honest estimates creators can actually use
						when planning videos, choosing topics, and thinking about their audience.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- What the tool offers — only what exists on this site -->
			<section aria-labelledby="offers-heading">
				<h2 id="offers-heading">What the Tool Offers</h2>
				<div class="cards">
					<article class="card">
						<h3>CPM &amp; RPM education</h3>
						<p>
							Plain-language explanations of what CPM and RPM mean, how they differ, and why
							both matter for creator revenue — on this page and across the
							<a href="/blog">blog</a>.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="card">
						<h3>Estimated earnings calculations</h3>
						<p>
							The simple formula behind every earnings estimate, explained step by step in
							<a href="#how-it-works">How it works</a>, so you can calculate estimated revenue
							from any view count and an assumed CPM or RPM value.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="card">
						<h3>Why earnings vary</h3>
						<p>
							Insights into how audience location, niche, content category, and advertiser
							demand can affect monetisation — see
							<a href="#transparency">Transparency and accuracy</a>.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="card">
						<h3>Scenario comparison</h3>
						<p>
							Work through hypothetical revenue scenarios — the same views at a different RPM,
							or a different view count at the same RPM — to compare outcomes on equal terms
							before you commit to a content direction.
						</p>
					</article>
				</div>
				<p>And throughout: a simple, creator-friendly experience.</p>
			</section>

			<!-- How it works -->
			<section id="how-it-works" aria-labelledby="how-heading">
				<h2 id="how-heading">How It Works</h2>
				<p>
					It starts with three numbers: <strong>views</strong>, <strong>CPM</strong>, and
					<strong>RPM</strong>. They sound similar, but they measure different sides of the
					same transaction.
				</p>
				<div class="cards">
					<article class="card">
						<h3>CPM — the advertiser side</h3>
						<p>
							CPM (Cost Per Mille) is what advertisers pay for 1,000 ad impressions. It
							reflects advertiser demand for your audience, niche, and ad inventory — before
							YouTube's revenue share.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="card">
						<h3>RPM — the creator side</h3>
						<p>
							RPM (Revenue Per Mille) is what a creator actually earns per 1,000 views, after
							YouTube's share. RPM is the more useful number for estimating your own income.
						</p>
					</article>
				</div>
				<h3>The estimate, in simple language</h3>
				<ol>
					<li>Pick a view count — a video you have published, or one you are planning.</li>
					<li>
						Choose an assumed RPM (or CPM) based on your niche, audience location, and past
						channel experience.
					</li>
					<li>Apply the formula:</li>
				</ol>
				<p class="formula">Estimated creator revenue &asymp; (Views &divide; 1,000) &times; RPM</p>
				<p>
					Worked example: 100,000 views at an assumed RPM of $4 gives (100,000 &divide; 1,000)
					&times; $4 = <strong>$400 estimated creator revenue</strong>. That figure is an
					<strong>estimate, not guaranteed earnings</strong> — your real RPM will depend on the
					factors below. Try a different RPM or view count in the same formula to compare a
					second, hypothetical scenario.
				</p>
			</section>

			<!-- Who it's for -->
			<section aria-labelledby="who-heading">
				<h2 id="who-heading">Who It's For</h2>
				<div class="cards">
					<article class="card">
						<h3>YouTube beginners</h3>
						<p>
							Learning what CPM and RPM mean before — or just after — monetisation, without
							needing an analytics background.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="card">
						<h3>Established creators</h3>
						<p>
							Sanity-checking revenue scenarios when planning a new series, format, or
							audience, and comparing niches on equal terms.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="card">
						<h3>Content strategists</h3>
						<p>
							Modelling hypothetical outcomes to inform topic, category, and
							audience-targeting decisions.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="card">
						<h3>Monetisation researchers</h3>
						<p>
							Anyone researching how YouTube monetisation works who wants clear definitions
							and transparent, reproducible estimates.
						</p>
					</article>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Transparency -->
			<section id="transparency" aria-labelledby="transparency-heading">
				<h2 id="transparency-heading">Transparency and Accuracy</h2>
				<p>
					Estimates are only as good as the assumptions behind them. Actual YouTube earnings
					vary — often significantly — based on factors including:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Viewer geography</strong> — advertiser demand and rates differ by country
						and region.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Niche and content category</strong> — some topics attract higher-value
						advertisers than others.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Monetised playbacks</strong> — not every view shows an ad, so views and ad
						impressions are not the same.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Ad formats and watch behaviour</strong> — format mix, video length, and
						viewer engagement affect revenue.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Seasonality</strong> — advertiser spending typically rises and falls
						through the year.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>YouTube's revenue share</strong> — the split between YouTube and the
						creator is already reflected in RPM, but not in raw CPM.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					This site works from the numbers <em>you</em> choose. It does not access your private
					YouTube Analytics data, and it does not claim to — the explanations and examples here
					simply apply the formula above to stated assumptions, so you can judge whether those
					assumptions are realistic for your channel.
				</p>
			</section>

			<!-- Disclaimer -->
			<section id="disclaimer" aria-labelledby="disclaimer-heading">
				<div class="disclaimer" role="note" aria-label="Important disclaimer">
					<h2 id="disclaimer-heading">Important Disclaimer</h2>
					<p>
						YouTube CPM Bot is an <strong>independent tool</strong> and is
						<strong>
							not affiliated with, endorsed by, or officially associated with YouTube or
							Google
						</strong>.
					</p>
					<p>
						All CPM, RPM, and earnings figures discussed on this site are estimates based on
						assumed values. They are <strong>not guarantees of actual income</strong>. Always
						rely on your own YouTube Studio analytics for your channel's real performance.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="actions">
					<a class="btn btn-primary" href="/blog">Explore the blog</a>
					<a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/">Back to home</a>
				</div>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
